# Meerkat TaskPanel supervisor — generated by `install.sh`.
#
# The ping layer runs every minute: it drops registry entries whose worker pid
# is gone. It reads no board, claims no card and starts no LLM turn. The
# continue layer runs every {{CONTINUE_INTERVAL}} seconds: it wakes the host's
# headless agent with a bounded `继续` prompt, and that turn runs the
# deterministic entries (patrol → workflow → poll).
#
# Load with:  crontab <scheduling>/cron/meerkat-taskpanel.cron
# Logs append to {{LOG}}.
#
# Each line names the ABSOLUTE node that rendered this file — never
# `/usr/bin/env node`, which cron resolves against its own minimal PATH (typically
# `/usr/bin:/bin`, holding no nvm and no Homebrew directory) and which therefore
# exits 127 on any machine whose node lives only there.
#
# The `PATH=` line below is what the same problem needs for everything a tick
# *derives*: the continue turn starts the host CLI by name, and so does the wake
# glue's `exec claude` and the worker's own process tree. It prepends those
# directories to cron's default rather than replacing it, so a child that asks
# for `sh` or `git` still finds them. `PATH=` is a cron *setting line*: it
# applies to every line after it. Via `crontab <file>` that is just this file;
# `install.sh --supervisor=enable` instead appends the lines inside a managed
# marker block, so there the PATH also reaches any line you add below that block
# (and is removed with it) — see docs/scheduling.md §Anywhere — cron.
#
# The interpreter and the directories are version-pinned: after a node or CLI
# upgrade, run `install.sh --force` to refresh this file and re-run `crontab`.

PATH={{UNIT_PATH_CRON}}

{{PING_CRON}} {{NODE}} {{SUPERVISOR}} --host {{HOST}} --agent {{AGENT}} --ping-only --once --log {{LOG}} --state {{STATE}} >/dev/null 2>&1
{{CONTINUE_CRON}} {{NODE}} {{SUPERVISOR}} --host {{HOST}} --agent {{AGENT}} --continue-only --once --log {{LOG}} --state {{STATE}} >/dev/null 2>&1
